8671875, -0.0014028549194335938, -0.0290679931640625, 0.00209808349609375, 0.023284912109375, -0.000446319580078125, -0.016265869140625, 0.0118560791015625, -0.01070404052734375, -0.01177215576171875, 0.03533935546875, 0.00881195068359375, 0.037078857421875, -0.0037364959716796875, 0.0052337646484375, -0.0156097412109375, 0.0199432373046875, 0.0223846435546875, 0.0038394927978515625, 0.016448974609375, -0.029022216796875, -9.489059448242188e-05, 0.00760650634765625, -0.007686614990234375, -0.0179290771484375, 0.0099334716796875, 0.0019121170043945312, 0.0208740234375, -0.03192138671875, -0.00281524658203125, -0.014801025390625, 0.01727294921875, 0.0142364501953125, -0.00873565673828125, 0.004230499267578125, -0.00506591796875, -0.0301513671875, 0.02728271484375, -0.0273895263671875, -0.0216522216796875, 0.0205841064453125, 0.0031890869140625, 0.0147857666015625, 0.01244354248046875, 0.02783203125, 0.034271240234375, -0.01003265380859375, -0.01076507568359375, -0.0026607513427734375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose  \nTo develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance (QA).\n\nMaterials and Methods  \n\nOverview of Workflow  \nThis retrospective study assessed a two-stage AI pipeline for structuring and QA of radiology reports. The system first segmented reports into anatomical sections using a rule-based agent, then refined assignments and performed QA tasks using a local large language model (LLM) agent. Performance was evaluated by radiologist review of a stratified sample of reports.\n\nStudy Design and Data  \nA total of 638 radiology reports from CT chest, abdomen, and pelvis exams, dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists at a single institution in 2023\u20132024, were included. Reports were randomly sampled for evaluation.\n\nMulti-Agent AI Pipeline Architecture  \n\nRule-Based Agent  \nThe first agent used regular expressions (regex) to identify and segment sentences into standardized anatomical sections (e.g., lungs, liver, kidneys). Regex patterns such as `r\"\\b(lungs?|pulmonary)\\b\"` and `r\"\\b(liver|hepatic)\\b\"` were iteratively developed by reviewing 100 randomly selected local reports. Patterns were refined to maximize recall (target >95% of anatomical section headers correctly identified) and minimize false positives (target <5% incorrect assignments), as measured by manual review on a validation set of 50 reports. Performance was tracked by calculating the proportion of correctly and incorrectly assigned sentences per section.\n\nLLM Agent and Prompt Engineering  \nA 7B-parameter Llama-2 model, running on-premises, was used to resolve ambiguous assignments and perform semantic QA checks. The LLM was prompted with institution-specific instructions. For example, initial prompts were: \u201cAssign the following sentence to the most appropriate anatomical section: [sentence].\u201d Iterative prompt engineering included adding explicit lists of local section headers and reporting conventions, e.g., \u201cUse the following section list: [list]. If uncertain, assign to \u2018Other\u2019.\u201d Radiologist feedback was incorporated by having two radiologists review LLM outputs on 30 validation reports, rating accuracy and providing suggestions. Prompts were revised until >90% agreement with expert assignments was achieved on the validation set.\n\nAgent Interaction and QA Tasks  \nA controller script orchestrated the pipeline: the regex agent generated preliminary section assignments, which the LLM agent reviewed and refined. The LLM also performed three QA tasks using prompt-based logic:  \n(a) Section mismatch: \u201cAre there findings in the Findings section not reflected in the Impression, or vice versa?\u201d  \n(b) Gender-anatomy conflict: \u201cDoes the report mention anatomical findings inconsistent with the patient\u2019s gender?\u201d  \n(c) Undocumented critical findings: \u201cIs there mention of a critical finding, and if so, is communication documented?\u201d  \nPrompts for each task were validated by comparing LLM outputs to expert annotations on 50 reports, with error rates (false positives/negatives) tracked and prompts revised to minimize errors (<10% for each task).\n\nEvaluation Design and Metrics  \nA random subset of 45 reports was selected for radiologist evaluation, stratified to include 23 flagged and 22 unflagged reports, reflecting the overall flagged/unflagged ratio. The sample size was chosen to provide a 95% confidence interval of \u00b115% for the primary outcome (correct restructuring rate), based on pilot data showing 65\u201375% accuracy. This interval was considered acceptable for a feasibility study, but is a limitation for precise performance estimation. Two board-certified radiologists independently assessed:  \n(1) Correctness of restructuring\u2014defined as all sentences assigned to correct anatomical sections, with no omission or fabrication of clinically important information. Clinically important information was operationalized as findings that, if omitted or misassigned, could alter patient management (e.g., new or significant lesions, critical findings).  \n(2) QA task performance\u2014each flagged issue was rated as true/false positive/negative.  \n(3) Omission or fabrication of clinically important information\u2014examples included missing documentation of a pulmonary nodule or erroneous addition of a finding.  \nDisagreements were resolved by consensus discussion; the frequency and nature of disagreements were recorded and categorized (e.g., section assignment, omission, fabrication).\n\nSystem Requirements and Deployment  \nThe system was deployed on a workstation with dual NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPUs, 256 GB RAM, and Ubuntu 22.04. LLM inference averaged 2.5 seconds per report; regex processing was <0.1 seconds per report. This hardware exceeds typical clinical infrastructure, potentially limiting deployment. Possible solutions include model quantization or use of smaller models, but these were not explored here. The system operated as a standalone batch processor and was not integrated into clinical workflow.\n\nResults  \n\nReport Structuring  \nThe system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format, retaining original content. Of the 45 evaluated reports, both reviewers agreed that 31 (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and 12 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%) had reviewer disagreement. Correct restructuring required all sentences to be assigned to the correct anatomical section, with no omission or fabrication of clinically important information. Cohen\u2019s kappa for restructuring correctness was 0.62, indicating substantial agreement. Disagreements most commonly involved borderline cases of section assignment (8/12), omission of minor findings (3/12), or ambiguous language (1/12).\n\nQuality Assurance Task Performance  \nConfusion matrices for each QA task, with 95% confidence intervals for sensitivity and specificity (calculated using the Wilson method), are shown below:\n\n| QA Task                  | True Pos | False Pos | True Neg | False Neg | Sensitivity (95% CI) | Specificity (95% CI) |\n|--------------------------|----------|-----------|----------|-----------|----------------------|----------------------|\n| Section Mismatch         |   10     |     2     |   31     |     2     | 0.83 (0.52\u20130.98)     | 0.94 (0.80\u20130.99)     |\n| Gender-Anatomy Conflict  |    2     |     0     |   43     |     0     | 1.00 (0.16\u20131.00)     | 1.00 (0.92\u20131.00)     |\n| Undocumented Critical    |    1     |     0     |   44     |     0     | 1.00 (0.03\u20131.00)     | 1.00 (0.92\u20131.00)     |\n\nFor section mismatches, false positives (2/14 flagged) were due to ambiguous language or non-standard section headers (e.g., \u201cupper abdomen\u201d instead of \u201cliver\u201d or \u201cspleen\u201d). False negatives (2/12 actual mismatches) occurred when findings were described in non-standard phrasing (e.g., \u201csmall hypodensity in right lobe\u201d not mapped to \u201cliver\u201d). For gender-anatomy conflicts, both flagged cases were true positives (e.g., mention of \u201cprostate\u201d in a female patient due to template error). For undocumented critical findings, the single flagged case was a true positive (e.g., \u201clarge pulmonary embolism\u201d not documented as communicated).\n\nAnalysis of Flagged Errors  \nOf the 90 reports flagged in the full dataset, 80 (12.5%) were for section mismatches, 7 (1.1%) for gender-anatomy conflicts, and 3 (0.5%) for undocumented critical findings. Section mismatches were categorized as:  \n- Omission of benign findings from Impression (54/80, 67.5%; e.g., \u201cmild atelectasis\u201d not summarized)  \n- Omission of potentially clinically significant findings (14/80, 17.5%; e.g., \u201cpulmonary nodule\u201d not in Impression)  \n- Misassignment to incorrect anatomical section (12/80, 15%; e.g., \u201cadrenal nodule\u201d placed under \u201ckidneys\u201d)  \nSources of error included language ambiguity (38%; e.g., \u201cright upper quadrant mass\u201d not mapped), atypical or missing section headers (27%), template errors (15%), and rare anatomical variants or complex sentence structure (20%). Of the 12/80 section mismatches involving potentially clinically significant findings, omission from the Impression could affect clinical decision-making. Gender-anatomy conflict errors were rare and resulted from template errors; none were clinically significant. All undocumented critical findings flagged were confirmed as true positives, but the small number precludes robust conclusions.\n\nLimitations  \nThis study is limited by its retrospective, single-institution design and potential overfitting to local reporting styles, particularly due to regex and prompt development on local data. This may limit generalizability and introduce spectrum bias. Only board-certified radiologists from the same institution generated and evaluated reports, introducing evaluator bias and possibly inflating agreement rates. The modest sample size for radiologist evaluation limits statistical power and may not capture the full range of reporting variability. No baseline or comparator (e.g., existing structuring or QA tools, or manual review) was included, limiting contextualization of performance; future work should include such comparisons. The lack of integration into clinical workflow and high hardware requirements (dual RTX 4090 GPUs) may limit real-world deployment; model optimization or cloud-based solutions could mitigate this. No external reviewers were included. To improve generalizability, future studies should use multi-institutional data, external reviewers, and prospective deployment.\n\nReferences and Context  \nPrior work on radiology report structuring and QA includes approaches using rule-based systems, classical NLP, and deep learning (e.g., Pons et al., Radiology 2016; Banerjee et al., J"}}